Fuzix for the Commodore Amiga
=============================

First target: a stock A1200 (68EC020, 2MB chip RAM, AGA, Gayle IDE,
Kickstart 3.1). Fast RAM is used when exec reports it. Tested in FS-UAE
only so far.

Licence
-------
Everything here is under the GNU GPL version 2, like the rest of the
Fuzix kernel (see LICENCE at the top of the tree); the Amiga programs in
utils/ likewise. Other people's work in it:

- the keymaps on the disks are made at build time from the X keyboard
  layouts (xkeyboard-config, MIT/X11 licence): mkxkbkeymap.py, notice in
  COPYING.xkb, which goes onto the disks as /usr/lib/keymaps/COPYING.xkb
- uae/vkey/virtual-keyboard-unstable-v1.xml is the wlroots protocol file
  (MIT licence, notice inside); used only by the test tools
- Fuzix's 8x8 font comes from Linux (GPL), as in every Fuzix port

Nothing from Commodore or Hyperion is in the tree or on the disks.
Kickstart is the user's own: Fuzix reads the Topaz font from the ROM in
the machine at run time. mkkeymap.py converts AmigaOS keymap files for
someone who owns them (make ... AMIGA_KEYMAPS=<folder>); disks built that
way are for that person only.

The free keymaps follow the AmigaOS ones: the country's layout, ` ~ top
left, \ | on the key next to Backspace, AmigaOS's Alt layer (Latin-1
symbols, accents on Alt+F-K). They can differ on a few keys from the
AmigaOS keymap of the same name (checked against Workbench 3.2.1: Swedish
on 2 Alt keys, German 5, Danish 6, others more). Polish, Turkish, Greek
and Russian are not made: the console fonts are Latin-1.

Installing on a real Amiga
--------------------------
  fuzix-floppy.adf     the install disk; also a whole Fuzix on one floppy
  fuzix-extras1-3.adf  the Extras disks: the rest of the commands, games,
                       manual pages (tar archives straight on the disk)

1. Boot the install disk. If there is a hard disk, Fuzix asks whether to
   install on it (or later: log in as root, type setup). It shows the
   disk's Amiga partitions and offers free space, a partition to replace
   (type its name), or the whole disk (type ERASE). AmigaOS partitions
   stay as they are. It asks which system starts by default, then adds a
   FUZIX partition (up to 32 MB) to the partition table, makes the file
   system, copies itself, and restarts.
2. Take the floppy out. The default system starts; for the other, hold
   both mouse buttons at power on and pick it in Kickstart's Boot
   Options. Kickstart loads the Fuzix loader from the partition table
   (stored there as the FUZIX partition's "file system", 1.3 KB); it reads
   the kernel from the partition's last 256 blocks through AmigaOS's own
   disk driver and starts it - only when FUZIX is the partition being
   booted (hdboot.S).
   Fuzix sees the AmigaOS partitions too: hdb1, hdb2... in the table's
   order; amigadir -d /dev/hdb1 lists one.
3. The first boot from the hard disk offers the Extras disks (or later:
   setup extras). Put each in and press Return.

Booting
-------
Images built by "make diskimage":

  fuzix.adf          boot floppy; the root file system is on the IDE disk
  fuzix-floppy.adf   the whole system on one floppy (no hard disk needed):
                     bootblock with an MBR partition table, the kernel,
                     and a 780K Fuzix file system from sector 200 with the
                     small tool set and setup (Standalone/filesystem-src/
                     fuzix-amigafloppy.pkg)
  fuzix-extras*.adf  the Extras disks (mkextras.py)
  fuzix.hunk         the hard disk loader with the kernel inside
  disk.img           a 40MB disk with everything installed, bootable on
                     its own (mkrdb.py: the layout setup makes on an
                     empty disk); released as the HDF for UAE
                     together with release/fuzix-amiga.fs-uae and
                     release/README.txt

The floppy drive is always hda (with or without a disk in it) and the
IDE disk hdb. If the boot floppy has a Fuzix partition it is the root
(hda1); otherwise the root is hdb1.

The kernel boots from a floppy. The bootblock
(bootblock.S) reads the kernel through trackdisk, notes chip and fast RAM
from exec's memory list, stops the drive motor, shuts the OS down,
copies the kernel to $400 and jumps to it. The root file system is the
first partition of the IDE disk (Images/amiga/disk.img, MBR partitions,
no byte swapping).

  make TARGET=amiga YACC=yacc diskimage     (with m68k-elf-gcc on PATH)

then uae/run.sh boots them in FS-UAE as a stock A1200, headless in cage.
uae/vkey types on the emulated keyboard with real key codes; key scripts
can "wait" for text on the screen (OCR). uae/tests/install.sh runs the
whole install unattended: empty disk, install floppy, Extras disks.

What works
----------
- Screens: four text consoles, tty1-tty4, each 640x256 PAL hires,
  80x32 characters, each with its own login; Amiga+F1..F4 switches
  (like flipping AmigaOS screens: the Copper shows another one)
- PAL and NTSC: an NTSC Amiga (or one switched to NTSC in Kickstart's
  Early Startup menu) gets 640x200, 80x25, and the 60Hz tick; seen
  from the beam at boot. The serial port follows the machine's crystal
- Colour: on AGA (A1200/A4000) 16 colours, the vt's ink and paper
  (ESC b / ESC c, IGRB), bold as bright; four bitplanes fetched 64 bits
  at a time, so the display leaves the CPU most of the chip bus. Older
  chipsets: one bitplane, white on black
- Scrolling copies nothing: the Copper starts each frame at the top
  text row and wraps to the buffer's start; the blitter clears rows
- Keyboard: CIA-A serial; every AmigaOS keymap (keymap command; Shift,
  Alt, Caps Lock, dead keys), US built in; cursor keys, F1-F10, Help.
  The keymaps are converted at build time from a Devs/Keymaps folder of
  your own Workbench: make ... AMIGA_KEYMAPS=<that folder>
- AmigaDOS disks: amigadir and amigaread list and copy files from
  AmigaDOS floppies (or .adf / hard disk images): OFS, FFS and every
  variant up to OS 3.2's long names (CFile's reader, adf.c)
- Battery clock: MSM6242 or RP5C01 at $DC0000 (A500+/A600/A2000/A3000/
  A4000, clock-port and accelerator clocks) as /dev/rtc: no date and
  time questions at boot; setdate -w saves the system time to it
- Serial: Paula on tty5, kernel messages mirrored there at 9600 baud
- Timer: vertical blank, 50Hz (PAL) or 60Hz (NTSC)
- IDE: Gayle (A1200/A600) through the generic devide driver, PIO; LBA
  or cylinder/head/sector drives (CONFIG_IDE_AUTO)
- Partitions: Amiga partition tables (RDB, rdb.c) and PC ones
- Floppy: DF0, 880K AmigaDOS-format tracks read and written through
  Paula's disk DMA (devfd.c); a track cache, whole-track writes; the
  motor goes off two seconds after the last use
- Fonts: Fuzix's own 8x8 font, or the Kickstart ROM's Topaz 8 (found in
  any ROM from 1.3 to 3.2); fontprefs picks one and /etc/rc loads it
- Boots to login and a shell in FS-UAE, with and without fast RAM

Outside this folder
-------------------
Everything Amiga-only is here: the drivers, rdb.c, the programs in
utils/, /etc/inittab and /etc/rc (put over the shared ones by
fuzix-platform-amiga.pkg), the floppy's package list. The shared tree
has only:

- Kernel/dev/devide*.[ch]: CONFIG_IDE_AUTO (LBA or CHS per drive),
  CONFIG_IDE_DRAIN, IDE_REG_ALTSTATUS (the 400ns status wait) and
  IDE_RESET_MS. Each is off unless a platform's config asks for it.
- Library/libs/utime.c (and utime as _utime in the syscall list): utime
  set every file to 1970 on big-endian machines. A fix for every port.
- Applications/util/umount.c: exited 1 after a good unmount.
- -std=gnu17 and -fpermissive in the 68000 rules: GCC 15 and later.
- Makefile: the amiga line in the list of targets.

